keel-overlay-coraza (0.1.2) trixie; urgency=medium

  * keel-rules.conf: an audit log, /var/log/coraza/audit.log. Nginx's
    error log said only "Access denied" and a unique_id, never which rule
    blocked. One JSON object a line for each blocked request and each
    4xx or 5xx answer but 404, the matches in ModSecurity's format ([id]
    [msg] [data] [severity]) for CrowdSec to parse later, with the
    client, host and URI, never a header (Cookie and Authorization,
    which Coraza cannot redact) or a body. The workers write it as
    www-data: file 0600, directory www-data:www-data 0700, made at boot
    by a tmpfiles.d entry. keel-overlay-coraza-logrotate.timer runs
    logrotate hourly on /etc/keel-overlay-coraza/logrotate.conf: daily,
    or at 100 MiB, copied and truncated. Depends on logrotate. A purge
    deletes the logs.
  * keel-exclusions.conf, read before the Core Rule Set: on Anubis's
    pass-challenge path only, CRS 931100 and 932130 no longer read the
    argument redir. Opened by IP, pass-challenge carried a redir naming
    the IP, and 931100 (IPv4) or 932130 (IPv6, its brackets read as a
    shell glob) scored 5 and blocked it, so no browser passed the
    challenge by IP. The path is compared whole, URL-decoded and
    normalised, so dot segments cannot carry the exclusion to another
    location. Every other rule, argument and path is unchanged.

 -- Marcos Mendez <mendez.foto@gmail.com>  Fri, 02 Oct 2026 04:00:00 +0000

keel-overlay-coraza (0.1.1) trixie; urgency=medium

  * keel.conf: coraza_delay_response_headers off. With the headers held
    for phase 4, a static file answered with gzip was an empty gzip stream
    while sendfile is on (Keel-Linux/libnginx-mod-http-coraza#2), so
    browsers got blank pages. The Core Rule Set still blocks in phase 2; a
    phase 4 deny resets the connection instead of answering 403. sendfile
    off cannot be set in the http context of Debian's nginx.conf. To be
    removed once libnginx-mod-http-coraza 0.21.0-0keel2 is installed.
  * The manifest declares hooks.state, /usr/lib/keel/overlays/coraza/state:
    keel 0.15.0 runs a state hook only when the overlay manifest declares
    it (Keel-Linux/keel#63, the handbook's erratum of docs/manifest-v1.md).
    Depends on keel (>= 0.15.0), the first keel that reads the key.

 -- Marcos Mendez <mendez.foto@gmail.com>  Thu, 01 Oct 2026 21:00:00 +0000

keel-overlay-coraza (0.1.0) trixie; urgency=medium

  * First release (handbook decision 0041, first implementation, step 6).
    Depends on libnginx-mod-http-coraza 0.21.0 and coreruleset 4.25.1,
    built by Keel (step 5); ships the overlay manifest of
    docs/manifest-v1.md as /usr/share/keel/overlays/coraza.yaml.
  * Installed disabled, the module not loaded: postinst removes the
    modules-enabled link dh_nginx made, on the first installation only;
    preinst keeps a link that was there before on a live system, never in
    an image build; postrm forgets that record on purge and abort-install.
  * /usr/lib/keel/overlays/coraza/state enabled|disabled links the module
    and /etc/nginx/coraza/keel.conf, tests, reloads, and checks that the
    workers live and the manifest's probe gets 403, rolling back
    otherwise: a rule Coraza refuses passes nginx -t and then kills every
    worker (upstream corazawaf/coraza-nginx#139). A failed reload rolls
    back too; it probes only once the workers of before the reload stop
    taking requests; runs are serialised with flock.
  * A dpkg trigger on coreruleset, the module and libcoraza runs
    `state recheck` after their upgrades, turning Coraza off rather than
    leave Nginx without workers. prerm remove takes both links away, so
    autoremoving the module or the rule set cannot break Nginx.

 -- Marcos Mendez <mendez.foto@gmail.com>  Thu, 01 Oct 2026 03:00:00 +0000
